{"id":"f12t2","name":"Products Bought Together","lang":"pyspark","entry":"solve","params":["spark","lines_df"],"limit_s":30,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>The category managers want to place products that are often bought together next to each other on the shelves. <code>lines_df</code> holds the lines of the receipts of all shops, with the columns <code>receipt_id</code>, <code>store</code> and <code>product</code> (strings). A product appears on one line per unit sold, so the same product can appear several times on a receipt, and product names were typed with inconsistent case and spaces (<code>\" Milk\"</code> and <code>\"milk\"</code> are the same product; use the trimmed lower-case name).</p>\n<p>Two different products are <b>bought together</b> in a receipt when both appear on it, whatever their quantities. For each store, the managers want the pairs of products bought together in the largest number of receipts of that store. A pair is written once, with the product that comes first alphabetically in <code>product_a</code>, and a pair seen in a single receipt is a coincidence and must never be reported.</p>\n<p>Your task is to return, for each store, its 3 best pairs, ranked by number of receipts (highest first); when two pairs have the same number of receipts, the pair that comes first alphabetically (by <code>product_a</code>, then <code>product_b</code>) gets the better rank. A store can have fewer than 3 pairs. Return the columns <code>store</code>, <code>rank</code> (integer from 1), <code>product_a</code>, <code>product_b</code> and <code>receipts</code> (long), sorted by <code>store</code>, then <code>rank</code>.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p><code>lines_df</code>:</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>receipt_id</th><th>store</th><th>product</th></tr><tr><td>r1</td><td>S1</td><td>Milk</td></tr><tr><td>r1</td><td>S1</td><td>bread</td></tr><tr><td>r1</td><td>S1</td><td>milk</td></tr><tr><td>r2</td><td>S1</td><td> Milk</td></tr><tr><td>r2</td><td>S1</td><td>Bread</td></tr><tr><td>r2</td><td>S1</td><td>tea</td></tr><tr><td>r3</td><td>S1</td><td>tea</td></tr><tr><td>r3</td><td>S1</td><td>milk</td></tr><tr><td>r4</td><td>S1</td><td>tea</td></tr><tr><td>r4</td><td>S1</td><td>bread</td></tr><tr><td>r5</td><td>S1</td><td>tea</td></tr><tr><td>r5</td><td>S1</td><td>milk</td></tr><tr><td>r6</td><td>S2</td><td>rice</td></tr><tr><td>r6</td><td>S2</td><td>oil</td></tr><tr><td>r7</td><td>S2</td><td>oil</td></tr><tr><td>r7</td><td>S2</td><td>salt</td></tr></table></div><p>the output should be</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>store</th><th>rank</th><th>product_a</th><th>product_b</th><th>receipts</th></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>1</td><td>milk</td><td>tea</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>2</td><td>bread</td><td>milk</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>3</td><td>bread</td><td>tea</td><td>2</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 30 seconds (pyspark)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 2 GB<br><b>[input]</b> DataFrame lines_df<br><b>[output]</b> DataFrame with exactly the columns <code>store</code>, <code>rank</code>, <code>product_a</code>, <code>product_b</code>, <code>receipts</code>, in this order; rows in the order given by the statement</div>","starter":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession, DataFrame, functions as F\nfrom pyspark.sql.window import Window\n\n\ndef solve(spark: SparkSession, lines_df: DataFrame) -> DataFrame:\n    # write your code here\n    return lines_df\n","hints":[],"summary_fr":"","tags":["consigne longue","auto-jointure","paires sans doublon","top N avec départage"],"minutes":22,"source":"Kata Lab","howto":"<h5>Où écrire</h5><ol><li>Écris dans l'éditeur à droite, fichier <code>solution.py</code>.</li><li>Complète la fonction <code>solve(spark, lines_df)</code>. Garde son nom et ses paramètres : ce sont les tests qui l'appellent, avec leurs propres données. N'écris pas d'appel à la fonction toi-même.</li><li><code>spark</code> est une SparkSession déjà prête : ne crée pas la tienne.</li><li><code>lines_df</code> est un DataFrame déjà chargé : ses colonnes sont celles de l'énoncé (onglet <b>Input</b> d'un test pour les voir).</li></ol><h5>Ce que tu dois renvoyer</h5><ol><li>Ta fonction doit <b>renvoyer</b> le résultat avec <code>return</code>. Un <code>print</code> ou un <code>show()</code> ne compte pas : il sort seulement dans l'onglet <b>Console Output</b>.</li><li>Le DataFrame renvoyé doit avoir <b>exactement 5 colonnes</b>, avec ces noms et dans cet ordre : <code>store</code>, <code>rank</code>, <code>product_a</code>, <code>product_b</code>, <code>receipts</code>. Une colonne en plus (une colonne de calcul oubliée), un nom différent ou un autre ordre et le test est faux, même si les valeurs sont justes. Finis par un <code>select(...)</code> de ces colonnes.</li><li><b>L'ordre des lignes compte</b> : trie exactement comme l'énoncé le demande (<code>orderBy</code> à la fin).</li></ol><h5>Lancer et vérifier</h5><ol><li>Spark tourne dans ton Codespace : au premier RUN TESTS de la séance, attends qu'il soit prêt (1 à 2 minutes). Ensuite chaque lancement prend quelques secondes.</li><li>Clique <b>RUN TESTS</b> (ou <code>Ctrl + Entrée</code>) : tes 2 tests visibles tournent.</li><li>Quand c'est bon, tu vois en vert <b>« Tests passed: 2/2. »</b>. Sinon, ouvre le test marqué <b>Wrong answer</b> : compare « Expected return value » et « Your return value », et clique <b>VIEW DIFF</b> pour colorer les cases différentes. <b>Runtime error</b> veut dire que ton code a planté : le message est dans le test.</li><li>Clique <b>SUBMIT</b> : ta note sur 100 compte aussi 6 tests cachés. Soumets dès que quelques tests passent, puis améliore et resoumets : c'est la dernière soumission qui compte.</li></ol>","files":null,"visible":[{"input":{"lines_df":{"__df__":{"schema":"receipt_id string, store string, product string","rows":[["r1","S1","Milk"],["r1","S1","bread"],["r1","S1","milk"],["r2","S1"," Milk"],["r2","S1","Bread"],["r2","S1","tea"],["r3","S1","tea"],["r3","S1","milk"],["r4","S1","tea"],["r4","S1","bread"],["r5","S1","tea"],["r5","S1","milk"],["r6","S2","rice"],["r6","S2","oil"],["r7","S2","oil"],["r7","S2","salt"]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["store","rank","product_a","product_b","receipts"],"rows":[["S1",1,"milk","tea",3],["S1",2,"bread","milk",2],["S1",3,"bread","tea",2]]}},{"input":{"lines_df":{"__df__":{"schema":"receipt_id string, store string, product string","rows":[["a","X","p"],["a","X","q"],["b","X","q"],["b","X","p"],["c","X","p"],["c","X","r"],["d","X","r"],["d","X","p"],["e","X","s"],["e","X","t"],["f","X","t"],["f","X","s"],["g","X","a"],["g","X","b"],["h","X","a"],["h","X","b"]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["store","rank","product_a","product_b","receipts"],"rows":[["X",1,"a","b",2],["X",2,"p","q",2],["X",3,"p","r",2]]}}],"hidden_n":6,"hidden":"","solution":""}